[NAME]
daykeep \- sum and track time in decimal days

[EXAMPLES]
.nf
.RB "$ " "daykeep .9 \- .1"
\&.2000
.RB "$ " "daykeep \-\-hm '09:00 \- 10:30' 11:00 \- 11:45"
1h 30m
2h 15m
.RB "$ " "daykeep \-s \-\-format=minutes \-f today.log"
135
.RB "$ " "daykeep \-\-now"
09761.4564
.RB "$ " "daykeep \-\-convert .5 +9 10:00"
2026\-09\-22 09:00:00 \-0300
2026\-09\-29 21:00:00 \-0300
09761.5417
.RB "$ " "daykeep \-t \-a work.log"
.fi
.PP
The last one tracks time interactively and keeps the entries in
.IR work.log ,
which
.B "daykeep \-f work.log"
sums.

[ENVIRONMENT]
.TP
.B TZ
The time zone for HH:MM input and for dates and times printed by
.BR \-\-convert ,
unless
.B \-u
is given.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, options must come before the operands.
.TP
.B DAYKEEP_EPOCH
Day 0, unless
.B \-\-epoch
is given; it overrides the config file.
.TP
.B DAYKEEP_DAY_LENGTH
Whole seconds per personal day (1 through 1000000000, default 86400), unless
.B \-\-day-length
is given.  Each setting independently overrides its config value.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
The directory holding
.IR daykeep/config .
.TP
.B DAYKEEP_NOW
If set, used as the current time instead of the clock: a full stamp such as
.B 09761.4564
or
.BI @ SECONDS
since the Unix epoch.  It is meant for tests.

[FILES]
.TP
.I ~/.config/daykeep/config
Settings, one
.I "key = value"
per line;
.B #
starts a comment.  Keys are
.BR epoch
(day 0, as for
.BR \-\-epoch )
and
.BR day_length
(whole seconds per day, as for
.BR \-\-day-length ).
The last occurrence of each key counts.  The file is read when either
setting needs it; overridden values of known keys are ignored.  Unknown
keys and malformed lines remain errors.  Overriding both settings bypasses
the file entirely.  Empty environment variables count as unset.
The file is
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/daykeep/config
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set.
.PP
Logs must be read or resumed with the epoch and day length used to write
them; neither setting is stored in the log.  Decimal input rounds to whole
seconds, and output to four decimal places.  Civil dates and HH:MM retain
their ordinary meaning.

[SEE ALSO]
.BR date (1)
